# Plan — etude-9uf.3: Delete the retroactive capture path

## What is being deleted and why

Three scripts implement the after-the-fact reconstruction the supervisor model
replaces:

| File | Lines | What it did |
|---|---|---|
| `scripts/dogfood-capture.sh` | 139 | Rebuilt five stage artifacts from the bead `design` field and `git show <sha>` AFTER the bead closed. |
| `scripts/dogfood-close.sh` | 146 | Orchestrated the close sequence: preflight, capture run, capture gates, terminal audit. |
| `scripts/dogfood-close_test.sh` | 689 | Fixture tests. **Only partly deletable — see below.** |

Under the worker-lane model every phase artifact exists at gate time and is
captured by `etude capture` as the phase completes, so there is nothing left to
reconstruct. Keeping them would leave two contradictory descriptions of how a
bead is recorded — the failure this epic exists to end.

### The test file is two test suites, and only one of them dies

`scripts/dogfood-close_test.sh` has two sections, and the bead's line count
(689) treats them as one deletable unit. They are not:

| Section | Lines | Subject | Fate |
|---|---|---|---|
| A (1–428) | 428 | `dogfood-close.sh`: happy path, gateless run, unpushed ref, allowlist bypass, capture/gate/audit sequencing, preflight guard. | **Delete** — its subject is deleted. |
| B (429–689) | 261 | `.beads/hooks/pre-push` ref classification: B1–B10 (etude-only exempt, code push audited, gap rejected, mixed refs, deletion-only, tags, empty stdin, unknown refs, missing audit script, and audit-exit-2 fails closed). | **KEEP** — its subject SURVIVES this bead. |

Section B does not test the close script at all. It runs `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK`
directly with synthetic stdin and an injected audit sentinel
(`DOGFOOD_HOOK_AUDIT_SCRIPT`), and references no close-script symbol. Its
subject is the hook this bead is *editing* and which stays live afterwards.

Deleting all 689 lines would therefore silently drop the only coverage of a push
gate that fires on every push in this checkout — including its fail-closed
branch (B10). That is a real behavior regression hiding inside a deletion, and
the acceptance's "the three scripts are gone" does not authorize it: the bead
scopes the deletion by what the retroactive path *is*, and Section B is not part
of it.

**So: extract Section B to `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`** with the harness helpers
it needs (`t_start`/`assert_exit`, the throwaway-repo fixture, the audit
sentinel), and delete the rest of the file. The Makefile target is renamed
accordingly rather than removed. Net deletion is ~428 lines from this file, not
689 — and the bead's stated line count is corrected rather than met by dropping
tests.

`scripts/dogfood-gate-capture.sh` is NOT in the deletion set. It remains the
bootstrap path for a hand-assembled gate record, which the supervisor runbook
documents and which this epic itself used before `etude gate` existed.

## The sweep

`grep -rl 'dogfood-capture\.sh|dogfood-close\.sh|dogfood-close_test'` currently
returns, outside the deleted files themselves:

| File | Treatment |
|---|---|
| `Makefile` | the `dogfood-close-test` target invokes the deleted file. **Rename** it to `pre-push-test` pointing at the extracted `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`, and update its `.PHONY` entry and comment. Not a bare deletion — the surviving suite must stay runnable by a named target or it will rot unrun. |
| `.beads/hooks/pre-push` | two mentions: a comment explaining the `refs/etude/*` exemption, and an error message telling the operator to "Close the listed bead(s) with scripts/dogfood-close.sh". **This hook is LIVE** — `git config core.hooksPath` resolves to `/Users/jv/projects/etude/.beads/hooks`, whose `pre-push` is byte-identical to the tracked copy, so it runs on every push from every worktree. Its BLOCKED message would send an operator to a script that no longer exists. Rewrite both to the current close flow. |
| `examples/summarize/README.md` (×2), `examples/summarize/walkthrough.sh` | all three are asides contrasting the generic example with "the tracker-coupled `dogfood-capture.sh`". The contrast still has a point; name the retired mechanism instead of the file. |
| `docs/plans/product/gate-reviewer-record-schema.md` (×2) | one cites `dogfood-capture.sh line 69` as evidence for a schema decision, the other names it in a byte-identical-output claim. These are historical justifications; reword to the mechanism, preserving the argument. |
| `scripts/dogfood-gate-capture.sh` | its header comment contrasts a gate attempt with "a stage capture (dogfood-capture.sh, run once post-close)". This script SURVIVES, so a comment naming a deleted file is a live wrong pointer, not history — reword to the mechanism. |
| `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit.sh` | mentions the close script in its own prose. Bead .4 replaces this file wholesale, so touching it here would create a conflict with .4 for no gain — **left alone deliberately**, and .4's acceptance covers it. |
| `.workmux/PROMPT-epic-9uf-supervised-lanes.md` | the operator's own kickoff prompt for this lane. Not repo source, not mine to edit. **Left alone.** |

Two deliberate exemptions above are the only ones, and each is named with its
reason rather than being silently skipped.

## Verification

- `grep -rn 'dogfood-capture\.sh\|dogfood-close\.sh\|dogfood-close_test'` returns
  exactly two files, both named as deliberate exemptions above:
  `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit.sh` (bead .4's file) and `.workmux/PROMPT-*`
  (not repo source). Any third hit is a missed sweep site and fails this bead.
- `make test` and `make lint` green.
- `make docs-check` and `make docs-reality` green.
- `bash examples/summarize/walkthrough.sh` still runs end to end — it is the file
  I am editing, so a prose edit must not have broken it.
- `make dogfood-audit` still runs (it is .4's to replace, not mine to break).
- No Makefile target references a deleted file: `make -n dogfood-close-test`
  fails with "No rule to make target".
- **`make pre-push-test` passes, all ten B-cases.** This is the load-bearing
  check of the split: the extracted suite must exercise the live hook exactly as
  it did before extraction, not merely parse.
- **Negative control on the extraction:** temporarily break one hook branch (make
  the `refs/etude/*` exemption fall through to the audit) and confirm
  `make pre-push-test` FAILS. An extracted suite that passes against a broken
  hook has lost its wiring — most likely `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` resolving to a path
  that no longer exists — and would be worse than the deletion it replaced.

## Risks

- **The pre-push hook is live, and it is on the path of every push.** An earlier
  draft of this plan asserted the opposite. A syntax error or a bad edit there
  does not produce a wrong message, it blocks pushes repo-wide, for every lane
  sharing this checkout — the blast radius is larger than this bead. Mitigation:
  the edit is confined to two message/comment strings and changes no control
  flow; verify with `bash -n`, confirm the diff touches no `if`/`exit`/test
  construct, and exercise it once by pushing after the change.
- **The deletion could have taken live coverage with it.** The bead names one
  689-line test file; two thirds of it tests the deleted script and one third
  tests a hook that survives. A line-count-driven deletion would have passed
  `make test` and `make lint` cleanly while removing the only tests of a live
  push gate. Mitigation is the extraction plus its negative control above.
- **Deleting ~713 lines removes the only executable description of the old
  protocol.** Git history keeps it, and bead .2 already removed the prose that
  described it; leaving the scripts would leave the repo able to *perform* a
  protocol its own docs no longer describe.

## Correction to this plan

Two errors in an earlier draft, both found by checking the tree rather than by a
seat, both recorded rather than silently amended.

The first: the sweep table omitted `scripts/dogfood-gate-capture.sh`,
and the Verification section asserted a grep result that draft would not have
produced. Re-running the grep against the current tree found the third hit. It is
recorded here rather than silently amended because the omission is the same class
of error this bead exists to remove: a document describing a state the repo is not
actually in.

The second: the Risks section claimed the pre-push hook was not installed. It is
— `core.hooksPath` points at `.beads/hooks`, so the tracked file IS the running
hook. That inverts the risk, from "a message nobody will read" to "a live gate on
every push in this checkout", and it is now recorded that way.

Both errors are the same class this bead exists to remove: a document asserting a
state the repo is not actually in.

The third, and the one that would have cost real coverage: the deletion set
treated `scripts/dogfood-close_test.sh` as a single unit because the bead does.
Reading it showed two independent suites, and its Section B tests the live
pre-push hook — which this bead edits and keeps. That is now an extraction, not
a deletion, and the bead's own line count is corrected rather than honoured.
